WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.180
You know, usually when we talk about a medical diagnosis, there's this expectation of precision.

2
00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.180
Oh, absolutely.

3
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.620
Like engineering or something.

4
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:12.580
You break your arm, the x-ray shows that jagged white line, and the doctor just points and

5
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:13.580
says, there it is.

6
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:14.580
Right.

7
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:15.580
It's binary.

8
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:16.580
Broken or not broken.

9
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.660
I mean, we like things to be visible, right?

10
00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.100
To be categorized neatly into little boxes.

11
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.100
It gives us this, well, an illusion of total control.

12
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.580
Yeah, but then you step into the world psychology and suddenly that x-ray machine is just totally

13
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:29.580
useless.

14
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.300
You aren't looking for a cracked bone.

15
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.340
You're looking for like a cracked identity.

16
00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:35.340
Yeah.

17
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.740
And that is the reality of the Cluster B personality disorders.

18
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.780
Yeah, it really is.

19
00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.900
So if you've ever found yourself trying to understand human behavior that just seems

20
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.200
completely chaotic, this is exactly what we are unpacking on today's deep dive.

21
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:56.060
We are navigating a diagnostic landscape where drama, erratic behavior, and intense emotion

22
00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.560
all kind of blur together into one overwhelming picture.

23
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:05.380
And that blur, you know, that's precisely why this specific cluster of disorders is so

24
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:08.500
highly scrutinized by clinical professionals.

25
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:14.260
If you are trying to navigate complex human relationships or you're just someone who's

26
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:18.600
fascinated by why people do the things they do, this is an incredibly high stakes area

27
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:19.960
to understand.

28
00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.400
Because they overlap so much.

29
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:22.400
Exactly.

30
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.680
These personality types constantly bleed into one another.

31
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.520
On the surface, the behaviors can look identical.

32
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.520
They really do.

33
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.960
I mean, you look at the surface and it's basically just a storm of intense emotion.

34
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.560
You can easily confuse one of these conditions with another.

35
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.400
Or even, and this happens a lot, confuse them with entirely different psychiatric conditions

36
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.720
outside of this cluster.

37
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:42.720
All the time, yeah.

38
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.360
So to actually understand these disorders, we can't just look at what a person is

39
00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:47.360
doing.

40
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:48.360
Yeah.

41
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.880
We have to separate them cleanly by looking at the invisible architecture underneath.

42
00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:53.880
The mechanisms.

43
00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:54.880
Right?

44
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.400
We need to look at their underlying triggers, their hidden motives, and the precise tempo

45
00:01:58.400 --> 00:01:59.960
of their emotional shifts.

46
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:00.960
Exactly.

47
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.560
We have to look past the symptom and find the mechanism.

48
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:08.360
And honestly, the best place to start looking for those mechanisms is with Borderline Personality

49
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.360
Disorder.

50
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.360
The big one.

51
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:11.360
The big one.

52
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.720
If there is one word that defines the foundational core of Borderline, it is instability.

53
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.840
But not just like the everyday instability we all feel when we're stressed at work,

54
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:21.840
right?

55
00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:22.840
No, no, no.

56
00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:28.040
This is a pervasive instability that acts like a gravitational pull on literally everything

57
00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.720
in the person's life.

58
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:35.960
We're talking about highly unstable relationships, a fractured self-image, and intensely erratic

59
00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:36.960
emotions.

60
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:37.960
Right.

61
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:38.960
All paired with marked impulsivity.

62
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.200
It touches every single aspect of their existence.

63
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.080
People with Borderline, they experience the world essentially without emotional skin.

64
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.080
Wow.

65
00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:48.080
Without emotional skin.

66
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:49.080
Yeah.

67
00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.280
The slightest friction feels like a third degree burn to them.

68
00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:57.080
And this leads to deeply painful internal experiences, like a profound terror of abandonment,

69
00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:01.840
severe identity disturbance, and just a feeling of chronic agonizing emptiness.

70
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.160
Which has to be exhausting.

71
00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.440
It is.

72
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.460
Because that pain is so overwhelming, it often manifests in recurrent self-harm or suicidal

73
00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:14.400
behavior as a way to regulate or just escape that immense internal pressure.

74
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.400
Okay.

75
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.860
Let's unpack this idea of emotional regulation because that directly connects to how this

76
00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:19.860
is treated.

77
00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:24.140
The gold standard for Borderline is Dialectical Behavior Therapy, or DBT.

78
00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:25.140
That's right.

79
00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.980
But what does that actually mean in practice?

80
00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.940
Like it's not just sitting on a couch talking about your childhood, is it?

81
00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:31.940
Not at all.

82
00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.420
DBT is highly structured and incredibly practical.

83
00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:40.180
The word Dialectical, it basically means holding two seemingly opposite trues at the

84
00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:41.540
exact same time.

85
00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:42.540
Okay.

86
00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.440
For someone with Borderline, the emotional storm capsizes them because they operate

87
00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:47.780
in absolute extremes.

88
00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:48.780
It's all black or white.

89
00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:49.780
Right.

90
00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:50.780
There's no gray area.

91
00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:51.780
Exactly.

92
00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.720
So DBT acts like emotional shock absorbers.

93
00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:57.840
It teaches the patient how to say, I am absolutely terrified that you were leaving me and I am

94
00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.080
safe in this room right now.

95
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.480
So validating the feeling but grounding the reality.

96
00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.480
Yes.

97
00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.960
It teaches distress tolerance so they don't have to resort to self-harm when that intense

98
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.760
wave of emptiness hits.

99
00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:15.520
So it's teaching them to like anchor themselves while the storm passes.

100
00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:21.000
Which honestly is a perfect segue into the first major diagnostic trap we see here.

101
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.000
Let's hear it.

102
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.920
When you see someone experiencing massive, intense mood swings, your brain might automatically

103
00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.520
jump to a different diagnosis entirely.

104
00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:34.240
Like the biggest trap with Borderline is confusing it with bipolar disorder.

105
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:35.240
Oh yeah.

106
00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:39.560
It is the most common mistake made by people just observing these behaviors.

107
00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.960
But this is where we have to apply that framework we mentioned earlier, you know, tempo

108
00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:43.960
and trigger.

109
00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:44.960
Tempo and trigger.

110
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:45.960
Yeah.

111
00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.360
If you just look at the mood swings, they look exactly the same.

112
00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.320
You have to look at the clock and you have to look at the environment.

113
00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:50.320
Okay.

114
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.240
I'm going to challenge you here to map this out for a second.

115
00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:57.040
Is the difference between Borderline and bipolar kind of like the difference between a sudden

116
00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:03.640
violent thunderstorm caused by a specific draft of air versus say a long sustained winter

117
00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:04.640
season?

118
00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:05.640
I love that.

119
00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:09.120
That thunderstorm concept actually maps perfectly onto the clinical reality.

120
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.120
Really?

121
00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:11.120
Yeah.

122
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.640
Let's look at the thunderstorm first, which represents Borderline.

123
00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:17.600
The mood shifts in Borderline are rapid and highly reactive.

124
00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.880
They are almost always triggered by an interpersonal event.

125
00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:21.880
Like a fight.

126
00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:22.880
Right.

127
00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:29.200
A perceived slight, a text message left on red, a minor argument with a partner.

128
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.400
That is the sudden draft of air that sparks the storm.

129
00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.640
And the tempo of that storm typically lasts for a matter of hours, maybe a day.

130
00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:38.640
Wow.

131
00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:39.640
Okay.

132
00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:43.480
So it's fast and it is a direct immediate reaction to the relationship dynamic in that

133
00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:44.480
very moment.

134
00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:45.480
Precisely.

135
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.040
We contrast that with the winter season, right?

136
00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:50.560
Which represents bipolar disorder.

137
00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.960
Bipolar episodes are sustained chemical shifts.

138
00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:53.960
Not just a reaction.

139
00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:54.960
No.

140
00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:58.160
We're talking about mood states, either mania or depression, that last for days, weeks,

141
00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:59.960
or even months at a time.

142
00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.120
And crucially, they are episodic.

143
00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:05.080
They aren't necessarily driven by a sudden relationship rupture.

144
00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:06.080
Okay.

145
00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:07.080
That makes sense.

146
00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.100
I mean, winter doesn't happen because you had a fight with your friend.

147
00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.240
Winter happens because the season change.

148
00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:12.240
Right.

149
00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.440
So the key to separating them is looking at the mechanics.

150
00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:18.880
Reactive, fast, and triggered by relationships equals borderline.

151
00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:22.880
Sustained, long-lasting, and episodic equals bipolar.

152
00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:23.880
Exactly.

153
00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:28.640
You are separating reactive emotional instability from a sustained neurochemical episode.

154
00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:29.640
Wow.

155
00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:30.640
Okay.

156
00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.880
So if borderline is an emotional storm caused by deep internal panic and a fear of abandonment,

157
00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:40.800
what happens when that same intense emotional energy is used not as a frantic cry for help,

158
00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.240
but like as a mechanism for control?

159
00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.680
Well, that flicks us into a different category entirely.

160
00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:46.680
Right.

161
00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:50.880
We move away from the rapid reactivity of borderline and into personalities defined by how they

162
00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.600
use the people around them.

163
00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.080
So let's look at narcissistic personality disorder.

164
00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:56.080
Yeah.

165
00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:59.440
The term narcissist gets thrown around so casually today to describe anyone who takes

166
00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:01.560
too many selfies or acts selfishly.

167
00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:02.560
Oh, for sure.

168
00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:04.240
Everyone's ex is a narcissist now.

169
00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:05.240
Exactly.

170
00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:09.640
But the clinical reality of narcissistic personality disorder is far more complex and specific than

171
00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:10.640
just a vanity.

172
00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:15.680
The core traits are grandiosity, a pervasive need for admiration, and a profound lack of

173
00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:16.680
empathy.

174
00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:17.680
Right.

175
00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:21.880
It's this fundamental sense of entitlement, a belief that they are inherently special,

176
00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:25.720
and a total willingness to exploit others to maintain that status.

177
00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:28.720
But there is a fascinating contradiction here in the source material.

178
00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:29.920
Oh, the paradox.

179
00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:30.920
Yeah.

180
00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:36.560
On the surface, narcissistic personalities project absolute superiority and entitlement.

181
00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:41.560
But underneath that armor, their self-esteem is actually incredibly fragile.

182
00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:43.440
I struggle to reconcile that.

183
00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.520
How can someone be so grandiose but possess such a fragile ego at the exact same time?

184
00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:54.040
It is the central paradox of the entire disorder because the grandiosity isn't a reflection

185
00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:56.800
of true grounded self-confidence.

186
00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.560
Because true confidence can handle a little criticism.

187
00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:00.560
Exactly.

188
00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.920
For the narcissist, the grandiosity is a mask.

189
00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:04.880
It's an elaborate defense mechanism.

190
00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:09.840
They project that entitlement precisely because their underlying self-esteem is deeply vulnerable.

191
00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:11.680
Okay, so it's a shield.

192
00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:12.680
Yes.

193
00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:15.600
If you offer even a mild critique, it doesn't just hurt their feelings.

194
00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.120
To them, it threatens to shatter their entire constructed reality.

195
00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:23.840
That's why they often react with such disproportionate rage what clinicians actually call a narcissistic

196
00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:24.840
injury.

197
00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.160
So they aren't exploiting people just for the fun of it.

198
00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:32.200
They need the admiration from other people to continuously prop up the mask.

199
00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.600
Because the foundation underneath is crumbling.

200
00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:39.960
They require external validation the way the rest of us require oxygen.

201
00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.720
That brings us to why treatment is exceptionally difficult.

202
00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:43.720
I bet.

203
00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:44.720
Yeah.

204
00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:49.120
The first line treatment for narcissistic personality disorder is psychotherapy.

205
00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:52.120
But clinical engagement is incredibly hard to achieve.

206
00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:55.640
Well, I would imagine that's directly tied to the empathy deficit.

207
00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:01.440
If the entire goal of psychotherapy is self-reflection and understanding your impact on others, a

208
00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:04.480
narcissist is fundamentally missing the tools to do that work.

209
00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.480
Psychotherapy requires vulnerability, you know.

210
00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:11.480
It requires admitting you have flaws and recognizing the pain you've caused other people.

211
00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:12.480
Which they can't do.

212
00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:13.480
Right.

213
00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:18.240
If your primary defense mechanism is projecting absolute perfection and you lack the empathy

214
00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:22.880
to care about the people you've exploited, sitting in a therapist chair and dismantling

215
00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.760
your own mask feels like psychological annihilation.

216
00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:26.760
Wow.

217
00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:28.040
So they just don't go to therapy.

218
00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:34.080
They rarely seek treatment unless a major life event like a massive divorce or a huge

219
00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.840
career failure forces the mask to slip.

220
00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:38.840
Okay.

221
00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:43.760
So narcissistic personalities exploit people to manage their own fragile self-esteem.

222
00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:46.840
But that introduces another major diagnostic trap, doesn't it?

223
00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:48.760
Because they aren't the only ones who exploit people.

224
00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:50.320
No, they certainly are not.

225
00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:56.180
We have to separate narcissistic personality disorder from antisocial personality disorder.

226
00:09:56.180 --> 00:10:00.040
If you just look at the surface behavior, the fact that someone is being manipulated

227
00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:02.880
or used, you really won't be able to tell the two apart.

228
00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:03.880
Right.

229
00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:06.640
We need to return to our framework of hidden motives.

230
00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:12.040
The surface action is exploitation, but the why is entirely different.

231
00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.620
So the narcissist exploits to be admired.

232
00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:16.600
They want the applause to feed the ego.

233
00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:18.840
Why does the antisocial person exploit?

234
00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:24.280
The antisocial person exploits with total cold disregard for whether anyone gets hurt.

235
00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:26.040
And they do not care if you admire them or not.

236
00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:27.440
But no need for applause.

237
00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:28.440
None.

238
00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:29.440
It's not about applause.

239
00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:33.640
It's about a pervasive disregard for the rules of society and the rights of others.

240
00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:38.640
They want power, they want resources, or maybe just simple amusement.

241
00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:42.200
And other human beings are just, well, objects in the way of getting it.

242
00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.560
Man, it's a very dark psychological profile.

243
00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:45.560
It is.

244
00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:53.240
We're talking about a core defined by deceit, impulsivity, physical aggression, irresponsibility,

245
00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:55.320
and this chilling lack of remorse.

246
00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:56.320
Oh.

247
00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:59.120
If they hurt you to get what they want, they simply don't lose sleep over it.

248
00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.920
And because the implications of this disorder are so severe, I mean, often intersecting with

249
00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:08.960
the criminal justice system, the clinical rules for diagnosing it are incredibly rigid.

250
00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:12.160
You cannot casually label someone with antisocial personality disorder.

251
00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:13.160
Right.

252
00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:14.160
There are specific criteria.

253
00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:15.520
There are two heart and fast rules.

254
00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:18.480
First, the individual must be at least 18 years old.

255
00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:19.480
Okay.

256
00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.420
Let me push back on that for a second because that feels arbitrary.

257
00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:25.640
What happens if you have a 16 year old who is clearly showing all these intense traits

258
00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:28.840
that deceit, the aggression, the complete lack of remorse?

259
00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:31.240
Why wait two years to call it what it is?

260
00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:32.240
